function sortArray(array):
    if length(array) <= 1:
        return array

    pivotIndex ← length(array) // 2
    pivotValue ← array[pivotIndex]

    left ← empty array
    right ← empty array

    for i from 0 to length(array) - 1:
        if i = pivotIndex:
            continue
        if array[i] < pivotValue:
            append array[i] to left
        else:
            append array[i] to right

    return concatenate(sortArray(left), [pivotValue], sortArray(right))

This recursive approach—commonly known as Quicksort—exhibits an elegant structural symmetry: each invocation partitions the problem into smaller, self-similar subproblems until reaching a trivial base case, then recombines the sorted fragments into a coherent whole. The average time complexity settles at O(n log n), with the partitioning logic ensuring that relative ordering propagates correctly through each layer of recursion.
